                <pb facs="0650" n="436" sameAs="frus:frus1871/pg436"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">No. 205.</seg></opener>
                <head>Mr. Moran to Mr.
                        Fish</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 256.]</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps">Legation of the United States</hi>,
                                <hi rendition="#italic">London</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1871-03-08"><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >March</hi> 8, 1871</date>. (Received March 23.)</dateline></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir:</hi> I have the honor to send herewith two
                    copies of correspondence, just presented to Parliament, respecting the departure
                    of Lord Lyons from Paris at the time of its investment by the Germans. His
                    lordship’s letter of the 26th ultimo will, no doubt, attract your attention, as
                    it gives his reasons for going to Bordeaux, and his explanation of the motives
                    which induced Mr. Washburne to remain at his post.</p>
                <p>I am, &amp;c.,</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>BENJAMIN MORAN.</signed></closer>
















